THE CONTROL CASE FOR "AN ITEM WITH NOTHING IN IT".

Three items in, three out, and the middle one keeps its comment. An HTML
comment renders nothing, so this item looks empty to a reader of the OUTPUT
and is not empty at all to the parser: the comment is a node, the item has a
child, and its text is the comment itself.

WHY THAT MATTERS ENOUGH TO PIN. Three shapes reach the writer looking alike,
and md must tell them apart:

    - <!-- note -->   a child that renders nothing   KEPT, this case
    -                 no child, written empty        KEPT as `-`,
                                                     format-keeps-an-empty-list-item
    - [ref]: /url     no child, cmark ate it         KEPT, recovered from source,
                                                     format-keeps-a-link-reference-definition-in-a-list-item

Only the middle two share a mechanism. `collectListItems` decides an item's
fate by asking whether the item node has any children, so this case is the one
that must NOT go down that path, and it is the control that says the question
is being asked of the right thing. A fix that instead asked "is the item's
text empty" would keep this item too — by luck, since a comment has text — but
would fall over on an item holding something that renders nothing AND yields
no text.

It also guards the comment itself. An item's content comes back through the
CommonMark writer, and a comment that lost its brackets would become ordinary
prose in the middle of a list.
